Comparable companies need similar economics, risk, growth, and accounting, not merely industry labels.|A mature software vendor differs from an unprofitable subscription startup.|Same sector guarantees comparability?|No|Yes|Only if listed
Transaction comparables reflect deal conditions, timing, control, and expected synergies.|A takeover multiple includes benefits unavailable to a minority buyer.|Apply it unchanged to any listed share?|No|Yes|Only in cash deals
Trailing multiples use historical metrics; forward multiples use forecasts and their uncertainty.|Price ₹100 with trailing EPS ₹5 gives 20×; expected EPS ₹10 gives 10× forward.|Lower forward P/E proves the forecast is reliable?|No|Yes|Only after guidance
P/E prices earnings; P/B book equity; P/S sales; PEG relates P/E to growth with convention-sensitive limitations.|Price ₹100 and EPS ₹5 imply P/E 20.|Can a low P/E reflect weak growth or risk?|Yes|No|Only during recessions
EV multiples pair enterprise value with operating metrics before financing costs.|EV ₹200 and EBITDA ₹25 gives 8×.|Divide enterprise value by equity-only earnings blindly?|No|Yes|Only for large firms
Cash-flow and sector multiples require matched claims and metric definitions.|Equity price divided by equity cash flow differs from EV divided by FCFF.|The key consistency check is…?|Numerator and denominator ownership claims|Only decimal places|Only fiscal month
Normalised earnings adjust justified distortions and accounting differences.|Profit ₹20 includes non-recurring gain ₹5: normalised ₹15.|Recurring expenses should be excluded merely to improve a multiple?|No|Yes|Only if management excludes them
Growth, risk, margins, and capital returns influence justified multiples jointly.|Two firms grow equally, but one reinvests much more for that growth.|Equal growth implies equal justified multiples?|No|Yes|Only at equal sales
Sum-of-the-parts values distinct businesses separately and reconciles central costs and claims.|Division values ₹100 + ₹80 − central-cost PV ₹10 = ₹170 before the equity bridge.|Ignoring central costs can…?|Overstate value|Understate revenue|Eliminate debt
NAV revalues assets and liabilities; liquidation value adds disposal costs, timing, and priority.|Assets realise ₹100, liabilities ₹70, disposal costs ₹10: residual ₹20.|Residual liquidation value is…?|₹20|₹30|₹100
Replacement cost estimates current cost of equivalent capacity, with obsolescence and economic viability considered.|A ₹100 factory could be replaced for ₹120 but earns inadequate returns.|Replacement cost necessarily equals intrinsic value?|No|Yes|Only for tangible assets
Private-company valuation addresses limited disclosure, owner dependence, capital structure, and transfer restrictions.|Owner compensation is adjusted to a defensible market-equivalent cost.|A standard discount applies to every private company?|No|Yes|Only if unlisted
Pre-revenue valuation uses milestones, scenarios, future economics, and funding needs with high uncertainty.|A startup's success scenario is balanced against failure and dilution.|A large addressable market alone establishes value?|No|Yes|Only in technology
Cyclical valuation normalises across cycles; distressed valuation models recovery, claims, and survival outcomes.|Peak commodity earnings make a stock appear cheap on current P/E.|A low peak-earnings P/E necessarily means undervaluation?|No|Yes|Only for miners
Financial-company valuation reflects regulatory capital, credit losses, and equity cash capacity; generic industrial EV methods may misfit.|Bank growth requires additional equity capital to support assets.|Treating deposits like ordinary industrial debt blindly is…?|Potentially misleading|Always correct|Required by DCF
Intangible valuation isolates asset-related cash, royalty savings, or excess earnings without double counting.|A brand saves an assumed licensing fee over its economic life.|Its value should reflect…?|After-tax benefits, risk, and useful life|Only advertising spend|Only popularity
Control and marketability effects depend on rights, achievable changes, liquidity, and transaction facts.|A controlling buyer can change operations while a minority owner cannot.|A universal fixed control premium is always justified?|No|Yes|Only for private firms
Business value estimates benefits; transaction price also reflects bargaining, financing, strategic alternatives, and deal terms.|A buyer pays above stand-alone value to capture buyer-specific synergies.|Price above stand-alone value necessarily proves irrationality?|No|Yes|Only in auctions
